{
 "id": "reference-angles",
 "title": "Reference angles and degrees-minutes",
 "summary": "Find the reference angle of any angle and switch between decimal degrees and degrees-minutes-seconds.",
 "objectives": [
  "Name the quadrant of an angle in standard position",
  "Find the reference angle in each quadrant",
  "Convert between decimal degrees and degrees-minutes-seconds"
 ],
 "prerequisites": [],
 "lesson": [
  {
   "id": "ra-b01",
   "type": "text",
   "md": "# Reference angles\n\nAn angle in **standard position** starts on the positive $x$-axis and turns counterclockwise (positive) or clockwise (negative). Where it stops, its **terminal side**, decides almost everything about its trig values. The **reference angle** $\\theta'$ is the acute angle between the terminal side and the **$x$-axis**, never the $y$-axis."
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b02",
   "type": "predict",
   "question": "What is the reference angle of $150^\\circ$?",
   "options": [
    {
     "md": "$30^\\circ$",
     "correct": true,
     "why": "$180^\\circ-150^\\circ=30^\\circ$: measure to the nearest part of the $x$-axis."
    },
    {
     "md": "$60^\\circ$",
     "correct": false,
     "why": "That is the angle to the $y$-axis. Reference angles are always measured to the $x$-axis."
    },
    {
     "md": "$150^\\circ$",
     "correct": false,
     "why": "A reference angle is always acute, between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$."
    }
   ],
   "explanation": "The picture below shows why: the terminal side of $150^\\circ$ is $30^\\circ$ short of the negative $x$-axis."
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b03",
   "type": "plot",
   "title": "$150^\\circ$ and its reference angle",
