   ],
   "caption": "The terminal side stops in Quadrant II, $30^\\circ$ above the negative $x$-axis."
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b04",
   "type": "table",
   "headers": [
    "Quadrant",
    "Angle $\\theta$",
    "Reference angle $\\theta'$",
    "Example"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "I",
     "$0^\\circ<\\theta<90^\\circ$",
     "$\\theta'=\\theta$",
     "$40^\\circ\\to 40^\\circ$"
    ],
    [
     "II",
     "$90^\\circ<\\theta<180^\\circ$",
     "$\\theta'=180^\\circ-\\theta$",
     "$150^\\circ\\to 30^\\circ$"
    ],
    [
     "III",
     "$180^\\circ<\\theta<270^\\circ$",
     "$\\theta'=\\theta-180^\\circ$",
     "$225^\\circ\\to 45^\\circ$"
    ],
    [
     "IV",
     "$270^\\circ<\\theta<360^\\circ$",
     "$\\theta'=360^\\circ-\\theta$",
     "$305^\\circ 40'\\to 54^\\circ 20'$"
    ]
   ],
   "caption": "Reference angle by quadrant",
   "firstColumnIsHeader": true
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b05",
   "type": "callout",
   "kind": "tip",
   "title": "Coterminal first",
   "md": "If the angle is negative or larger than $360^\\circ$, add or subtract $360^\\circ$ until it lands in $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$; that **coterminal** angle has the same terminal side. Then use the table: $-210^\\circ+360^\\circ=150^\\circ$, so the reference angle is $30^\\circ$."
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b06",
   "type": "example",
   "title": "Reference angle of $305^\\circ 40'$",
   "problem": "Find the reference angle of $305^\\circ 40'$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "md": "The angle is between $270^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$, so the terminal side is in **Quadrant IV**."
    },
    {
     "md": "Use $\\theta'=360^\\circ-\\theta$."
    },
    {
     "md": "Borrow one degree so the minutes can be subtracted: $360^\\circ=359^\\circ 60'$.",
     "note": "Minutes go up to $60$, not $100$."
    },
    {
     "md": "$359^\\circ 60'-305^\\circ 40'=54^\\circ 20'$."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$54^\\circ 20'$"
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b07",
   "type": "text",
   "md": "## Degrees, minutes and seconds\n\nOne degree splits into $60$ minutes and one minute into $60$ seconds: $1^\\circ=60'$ and $1'=60''$. To go from minutes to decimal degrees, **divide by 60**; to go back, **multiply the decimal part by 60**."
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b08",
   "type": "math",
   "tex": "35^\\circ 15' = 35^\\circ+(\\tfrac{15}{60})^\\circ = 35.25^\\circ",
   "label": "Minutes to decimal degrees"
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b09",
   "type": "example",
   "title": "$72.41^\\circ$ in degrees and minutes",
   "problem": "Convert $72.41^\\circ$ to degrees and minutes, to the nearest minute.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "md": "Keep the whole degrees: $72^\\circ$."
    },
    {
     "md": "Multiply the decimal part by $60$: $0.41\\times 60=24.6'$."
    },
    {
     "md": "Round to the nearest minute: $24.6'\\approx 25'$."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$72^\\circ 25'$"
  },
  {
   "id": "ra-b10",
   "type": "spot",
   "intro": "A classmate converted $48^\\circ 30'$ to decimal degrees. Which line has the mistake?",
   "lines": [
    "$48^\\circ 30'=48^\\circ+30'$",
    "$30'=\\tfrac{30}{100}^\\circ=0.30^\\circ$",
    "$48^\\circ 30'=48.30^\\circ$"
   ],
   "wrongIndex": 1,
   "explanation": "A degree has $60$ minutes, not $100$: $30'=\\tfrac{30}{60}^\\circ=0.5^\\circ$, so $48^\\circ 30'=48.5^\\circ$."
  },
